@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Reinforcement Learning — Selection Map (learning from outcomes)
' Synthesis of the classical RL primers; the LLM-era application sits at Level 6
' Level:  1

actor "Human expert\nreward, not labels" as H

component "Temporal difference\nlearn a value function" as TD <<app>>
component "Q-learning\nlearn action values, off-policy" as Q <<app>>
component "Policy gradient\noptimize the policy directly" as PG <<host>>
component "Behaviour cloning\nimitate an existing script" as BC <<async>>
database  "Policy\nstate to action" as POL <<db>>

H ARROW_OPTIONAL BC : a decent teacher already exists —\ncheap cold start
H ARROW_MAIN TD : credit a long game to\nintermediate states
TD ARROW_MAIN Q
H ARROW_MAIN PG : stochastic or continuous actions
BC ARROW_MAIN POL
Q ARROW_MAIN POL
PG ARROW_MAIN POL

note bottom of POL
  **The problem RL solves is delayed credit:** a game
  won in 60 moves gives one number, and TD's answer
  is to bootstrap — predict from the next prediction
  (""temporal-difference-learning.puml"").
  ----
  **Start by cloning, not exploring.** If a scripted
  heuristic already plays adequately, behaviour
  cloning gives a warm start for a fraction of the
  self-play budget, and the script remains available
  as an explainer afterwards.
  ----
  **Ladder note.** These primers are Level 1. Running
  a reward loop against a pretrained model's weights
  is Level 6 — ""_spec/study-cases/6-post-training/rl-verifiable-rewards.puml""
  and ""_spec/study-cases/6-post-training/reinforcement-fine-tuning-pipeline.puml"".
end note
@enduml
